<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/expert/textual-criticism-and-editing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Textual criticism is <strong>the detective science of reconstructing texts from imperfect
    copies</strong> — comparing manuscripts, mapping inherited errors, deciding readings. Every
    classic you read (Homer, the Bible, Shakespeare) is a reconstruction built by this
    subfield's invisible labour, and every edition you buy embodies its choices.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="copies, family trees, famous cruxes, and the digital turn" widget="equationStep" config={textualCfg} />

  <h2>The craft and its judgements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lectio difficilior potior:</strong> the harder reading is likely the original — scribes smooth difficulties, so awkwardness smells authentic. One of several working principles, never absolute.</li>
    <li><strong>Recension and conjecture:</strong> when evidence fails, editors conjecture emendations — marked with brackets in scholarly editions. Every bracket is an admission and an argument.</li>
    <li><strong>Authorial versions complicate everything:</strong> Dickinson's variants, Yeats's revisions, Kafka's unfinished manuscripts — modern editing asks "WHICH text?" (final intention, earliest version, all versions?) — the genetic editing approach presents the composition itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters beyond classics:</strong> scripture debates (the Jehovah insert in 1 John 5:7), legal document authentication, and the provenance of any historical claim — textual criticism is the general science of transmission.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Maas, Textual Criticism; Ehrman, Misquoting Jesus (2005); the Oxford Shakespeare and Nestle-Aland NT editions as products.</p>
</TopicPage>
